    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "$$C_{6}^{2}x^{4}y^{2}+C_{6}^{3}x^{3}y^{3}+C_{6}^{4}x^{2}y^{4}+C_{6}^{5}x^{1}y^{5}+C_{6}^{6}y^{6}=$$\n$$36x^{4}y^{2}+20x^{3}y^{3}+15x^{2}y^{4}+6xy^{5}+y^{6}$$\n\nThe coefficient of the $x^2y^4$ term is $\\boxed{15}.$\n\nTo find the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term in the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\), we use the binomial theorem. The binomial theorem states that\n\n\\[\n(x + y)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} x^{n-k} y^k.\n\\]\n\nFor the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\), we have \\(n = 6\\), so the general term in the expansion is\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{k} x^{6-k} y^k.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the coefficient of the \\(x^2 y^4\\) term, which corresponds to the term where \\(6-k = 2\\) and \\(k = 4\\). This is the term\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} x^2 y^4.\n\\]\n\nThe coefficient of this term is\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\binom{6}{2} = \\frac{6 \\times 5}{2 \\times 1} = 15.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of the \\(x^2 y^4\\) term is \\(\\boxed{15}\\).",
