    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The term of $(x+y)^6$ with the power of $x$ is given by $\\binom{6}{k}x^k$,\nwith the power of $y$ is given by $\\binom{6}{k-2}y^{k-2}$.\nUsing the property of binomial coefficients,\n$\\binom{6}{k}x^k \\cdot \\binom{6}{k-2}y^{k-2} = \\binom{6}{k} \\cdot \\binom{6}{k-2}x^k \\cdot y^{k-2}$.\nUsing the power rule,\n$\\binom{6}{k} \\cdot \\binom{6}{k-2}x^k \\cdot y^{k-2} = \\binom{6}{k} \\cdot \\binom{6}{k-2}(k-2)!k!x^k \\cdot y^{k-2}$.\nExpanding,\n$\\binom{6}{k} \\cdot \\binom{6}{k-2}(k-2)!k!x^k \\cdot y^{k-2} = (\\underbrace{k(k-1)\\cdot ... \\cdot 1}_{k!})\\text{Coeff}(x^k)y^{k-2} + (\\underbrace{(k-2)(k-1)\\cdot ... \\cdot 1}_{(k-2)!})\\text{Coeff}(x^{k-2})y^{k-2}$.\nFor there to be a term with the power of $x^2$ $({k=2})$, the term must have $k-2 = k-2$.\nFor there to be a term with the power of $y^4$ $(k=4)$, the term must have $k-2 = 4$.\nTherefore, the coefficient of this term is $\\boxed{\\binom{6}{4}} = \\binom{6}{2}$.",
